 3.1 Fix an interior time, write $A:=D_xf(t,\Phi_t(x_0))$ and $C:=J_t^{-1}J_t'$, and denote the columns of $J_t$ by $v_1,\ldots,v_d$. By the Leibniz formula [F8], differentiating each $d$-factor monomial with repeated binary product rules (induction on the number of factors) and distributing the finite sum gives $(\det J_t)'=\sum_{\sigma\in S_d}\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)\sum_{j=1}^d(v_j')_{\sigma(j)}\prod_{i\ne j}(v_i)_{\sigma(i)}=\sum_{j=1}^d\det[v_1,\ldots,v_{j-1},v_j',v_{j+1},\ldots,v_d]$. Since $J_t'=J_tC$, if $C_j$ is column $j$ of $C$ then $v_j'=\sum_{k=1}^d C_{kj}v_k$. Multilinearity [F11] expands the $j$th determinant into $\sum_k C_{kj}\det[v_1,\ldots,v_{j-1},v_k,v_{j+1},\ldots,v_d]$; for $k\ne j$ the repeated columns make the determinant zero by alternation [F11], while $k=j$ gives $C_{jj}\det J_t$. Hence $(\det J_t)'=\det J_t\operatorname{tr}(C)$ and [F7] gives $(\log\det J_t)'=\operatorname{tr}(C)$. Since $C=J_t^{-1}AJ_t$, $\operatorname{tr}(C)=\sum_{i,j,k}(J_t^{-1})_{ij}A_{jk}(J_t)_{ki}=\sum_{j,k}A_{jk}(J_tJ_t^{-1})_{kj}=\sum_jA_{jj}=\operatorname{tr}(A)$. The density formula in step 2.1 has constant numerator $p_0(x_0)$, so [F7] and the chain rule [F5] give $(\log p_t(\Phi_t(x_0)))'=-(\det J_t)'/\det J_t=-\operatorname{tr}(D_xf(t,\Phi_t(x_0)))$. This proves the identity for $0<t<T$. [F4, F5, F7, F8, F11, F12, step 2.1, algebra, induction]
 
-3.2 At the endpoints, step 2.1 gives the variational equation for $J_t$ on $[0,T]$, and [F1] makes its endpoint equations one-sided: $J'_{0+}=A_0J_0$ and $J'_{T-}=A_TJ_T$, where $A_r:=D_xf(r,\Phi_r(x_0))$. To differentiate the determinant there, use its finite Leibniz polynomial [F8]: as $t\downarrow0$, each matrix entry has expansion $J_{ij}(t)=J_{ij}(0)+t(J'_{0+})_{ij}+o(t)$, and as $t\uparrow T$ it has expansion $J_{ij}(t)=J_{ij}(T)+(t-T)(J'_{T-})_{ij}+o(|t-T|)$. Expanding each finite product in that polynomial, the terms linear in the increment are exactly the binary product-rule terms and all terms with at least two increments are little-oh of the increment. Thus the same finite expansion as in step 3.1 gives $(\det J)'_{0+}=\det J_0\operatorname{tr}(J_0^{-1}A_0J_0)=\det J_0\operatorname{tr}(A_0)$ and $(\det J)'_{T-}=\det J_T\operatorname{tr}(J_T^{-1}A_TJ_T)=\det J_T\operatorname{tr}(A_T)$. The determinants are positive by step 2.1, so one-sided differentiation of their logarithms [F7] gives $(\log\det J)'_{0+}=\operatorname{tr}(A_0)$ and $(\log\det J)'_{T-}=\operatorname{tr}(A_T)$. Using the density identity in step 2.1, $\log p_t(\Phi_t(x_0))=\log p_0(x_0)-\log\det J_t$, therefore its right derivative at $0$ and left derivative at $T$ are respectively $-\operatorname{tr}(A_0)$ and $-\operatorname{tr}(A_T)$. These are exactly the stated endpoint identities. [F1, F4, F7, F8, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
